<h1 style=3D"font-size:26px;line-height:1.2;">Accessible colour systems</h1>
<p>Hi Anna,</p>
<p>This week, we=E2=80=99re looking at practical ways to test accessible colo=
ur systems=E2=80=94and make those checks part of everyday design work.</p>
<p>Start with the combinations people actually encounter: text on surfaces, l=
inks within paragraphs, and focus indicators against adjacent colours. Test d=
efault, hover, disabled, and error states rather than checking your palette i=
n isolation. Where colour carries meaning, add a label, icon, or pattern so t=
he message survives without it.</p>
<h2 style=3D"font-size:20px;">Featured case study</h2>
<p>Our featured case study follows the redesign of the North Harbour library =
catalogue, exploring how accessible colour decisions fit into a service peopl=
e use to search, browse, and find their next read.</p>
<p>For your next review, bring a contrast checker alongside a short list of r=
eal tasks. Passing a check is useful; making the interface understandable is =
the goal.</p>
<p>Thanks for reading,<br>The Layout Letter editorial team</p>
